t nearly as  strong as we believed employment was not  as strong as we believed how does that  play into this idea it plays in in I  mean in a lot of different ways but I'll  give you a couple examples  is you know the point that you made  about supply chains one of the things  that the Western world has been doing  basically over the last 40 years is  overall growth has been slowing down  growth hasn't been nearly as impressive  as it was you know in the heyday of the  Industrial Revolution or the post-war  boom and so we've been trying to  essentially do two things one spend way  more money way more public money  basically and take on way more debt in  order to sort of create the sense that  we're still growing quickly and to we're  trying to wring out sort of maximal  efficiencies everywhere we can find them  in order to again sort of Goose growth  and with supply chains and you know  things like our basic supplies of  medical equipment and masks and so on  you can see the danger in in the second  trend where you're you know you're so  focused on efficiency because you know  that you know you won't get strong  growth without maximal efficiency that  you aren't actually prepared to be  flexible when a real crisis comes along  and turns out that efficiency isn't the  only good it's also good to have  stockpiles of medical equipment so  that's one one sort of pattern and  another pattern is just the way sort of  large bureaucracies by their nature are  built to pursue safety rather than  innovation and that's not a bad thing  per se obviously you want the FDA and  the CDC to be focused on making sure  that medicine is safe and vaccines are  safe and so on but it makes them  inflexible not particularly adaptable  and you end up in this case with the  sort of temporary catastrophe like the  kind we had with testing in this country  over over the critical two months when  we missed the opportunity to contain the  virus where the bureaucracies default  mode is sort of t