d I also think you  could say in at a larger level that  there are ways in which generally the  economies of East Asia do look a little  less decadent generally than well  certainly than Western Europe but also  in the u.s. in terms of sort of their  general willingness to sort of embrace  innovation they're sort of a state  capacity this term of art that people  use to refer to you know how good a  government is and getting things done  state capacity seems like it's been in  decline in the US over the last few  decades and maybe less so in East Asia  so there's a lot of different dynamics  going on so as we see in the in the  United States even though at the country  level we could say we are certainly one  of  richest countries in the world we have  achieved a degree of what you describe  as decadence the reality about  inequality means that for a large number  of people only maybe a sliver of some of  these benefits are actually reaching  them when you look at you know wage  growth over the last 40 years and how it  disproportionately has been for a small  upper echelon of the economy so to speak  or workers as the case may be what is  the relationship between a country that  top level has achieved this status that  you identify to the degree to which it's  uniformly or not uniformly distributed  amongst the people I think it depends I  think I think there are a couple  different ways that you can get  inequality and one is more decadent and  one is less decadent right so if you  look at a period like late nineteenth  century America you have soaring  inequality you have the year of the  robber barons the great fortunes of the  Rockefellers and Carnegie's were being  made and that created all kinds of  social problems that eventually sort of  led to the labor movements of populist  and progressive you're and the New Deal  but it wasn't an inequality that really  reflected decadence it was an inequality  that reflected this age of innovation  where you had sudden innovations a few  people were capturing the returns and it  took a while for those returns to spread  throughout the population our own era  the last 30 years or so it looks like  that a little bit with Silicon Valley  where you have you know Internet  innovation does occur Berg's and and so  on get rich and it doesn't spread out to  the rest of society but a lot of what  we've seen outside the internet economy  is a steady stagnation slowing rates of  growth where rich people aren't getting  rich by embracing innovative new  technologies they're getting rich just  through the power of investments and  compound interest and so on and this is  Thomas Piketty this is sort of the  argument it says this is not this is the  more libertarian in a way version of the  argument that he made in his book  capital in the 21st century where he  basically said you know as societies  sort of as growth slows down and  societies get older and people have see  where kid  great fortunes become more and more  consolidated and rich people live off  ramps and that kind of inequality is  more decadent and more stagnant and less  likely to ultimately benefit society as  a whole than the inequality you get when  you know people are building the  Transcontinental Railroad and a lot of  opportunists are getting rich off that  innovation one of the things you talk  about in the book is the degree to which  a lot of the same political arguments  just continue to be had over and over  again with no real progress once a  country reaches this level of decadence  and I think we can intuitively see that  if you follow politics a lot of issues  that are merely political footballs you  know debt and deficit just kind of  tossing it back and forth what should  the role of the United States military  be around the world do low taxes on the  rich trickle down or do we need to man  sites that you know a lot of these that  it's the same arguments over and over  and over again can you talk about why  the 