t aren't particularly  innovative and so on you know more  people living their lives online and  instead of instead of getting married  and starting families you can imagine a  lot of ways in which this deepens  decadence potentially even as it offers  a chance to pull out of it a couple  weeks ago I interviewed Brian Greene and  one of the things we talked about  because it kind of mixes in well with a  few different things he deals with was  this idea that that I have which  increasingly I think is naive that if  there was some really life-altering  moment like for example intelligent  alien life arrives right something  really transcendent that it would  finally get humans to do away with these  differences that that as you talk about  often or the difference between a thirty  six and a thirty nine point six percent  top tax rate or whatever we would  finally get past all this stuff but I  have to say that the the fiasco that has  been the reaction to coronavirus where  you have people who you know all sorts  of conspiracies and you all we don't  even have to go into it you know it that  has made me think that even something  like intelligent alien life maybe for a  month would would get us to rethink some  of our differences and then it would all  just kind of go back to normal to the  way things have been I don't know well I  mean the amusing thing is that we have  literally had sort of footage of not  probably not actual aliens but at the  very least UFOs dropped in the middle of  the coronavirus crisis right you've had  the the Navy releasing footage of  unidentified flying objects and you know  except for a few people who I follow on  Twitter almost nobody has cared to be  clear that those are not being presented  as evidence of aliens right but but  regardless you would think that that  entire community would have paid more  attention there  right they're being presented but they  are they're very mysterious objects that  are then go beyond I think the sort